	Security

Reporting
---------

Do not open a public issue for a security problem. Email the address on the
maintainer's GitHub profile, or use GitHub private vulnerability reporting if it
is enabled here, and put [RVM security] in the subject.

Send what you found, the shortest guest program or disk image that triggers it,
the commit you tested, and what you expected instead. A proof of concept beats a
description, and an ASan or UBSan report with a stack trace beats both.

You get an acknowledgement within 72 hours. Anything that lets a guest reach the
host gets a fix or a documented mitigation within 14 days. Credit in the
advisory unless you ask for something else.


Scope
-----

RVM runs untrusted guest code by design, so the boundary that matters is guest to
host.

In scope: any guest instruction, MMIO access, virtio descriptor or FDT path that
reads or writes host memory outside the buffers set aside for it. Integer
overflow and unchecked length or index arithmetic in the device models, the
loader, the MMU and the virtqueue walker, because all of them consume values the
guest controls. A descriptor chain that makes the emulator loop forever or
allocate without bound. Path traversal through anything the guest can influence.
On Android, the guest reaching a file, socket or binder service it should not.

Out of scope: anything that needs a host process already running with elevated
privileges, since the Android app runs unprivileged. Bugs in the guest kernel or
in Debian. Side channels that need local access to the host CPU. RVM is a
software emulator with no constant time guarantees and does not claim to resist
cache or timing attacks from a neighbour process.


Threat model
------------

One thread owns one contiguous region of guest RAM. Everything else is a device
model reading and writing that region through bus_load() and bus_store().

Every length, offset and index that comes from the guest is bounds checked
against the region it indexes. Virtqueue descriptors are the sharp edge here:
addr and len come straight out of guest memory, so the walker has to reject a
chain that leaves RAM, that overlaps itself, or that is longer than the queue.

Device state has a fixed size and is allocated once. No guest input may cause an
allocation whose size the guest chose.

Host file descriptors are opened by the host, never by the guest. A disk path
comes from the command line or from the Android file picker, not from anything
inside the disk image.

No guest data is ever used as a format string. rvm_log() takes a literal format
plus arguments, and the console sink writes bytes.

MainActivity is the only exported component and its only intent filter is the
launcher one. Kernels and disk images enter through ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT, so the
user picks the file in the system picker. The resulting content URI is copied
into the app's private filesDir and treated as untrusted guest data from then on.
RVM never dereferences a raw path from an intent and never passes URI
permissions on to anyone else.


Versions
--------

main gets security fixes first. There is one release line and no long term
support branches. A security fix goes out as a patch release.


What is already in place
------------------------

Every CI run builds under AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer with
halt_on_error=1 and leak detection on.

The tree compiles with -Wall -Wextra -Wshadow -Wundef -Wpointer-arith
-Wcast-qual -Wstrict-prototypes -Wmissing-prototypes, and CI builds it optimised
with -Werror as well as syntax checking it, because constant folding at -O2 finds
warnings that -fsyntax-only does not.

librvm_jni.so is built with -fvisibility=hidden, so only the
Java_dev_rvm_app_RvmNative_* entry points are exported.

The APK is minified and resource shrunk, and the ProGuard rules keep only the
members the native side looks up by name.

No dangerous permissions. The manifest declares INTERNET and
ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, neither of which prompts, plus FOREGROUND_SERVICE which is
reserved for a future VM service and has no service behind it at the moment.
There is no storage permission; guest images are copied into private filesDir
through the storage access framework. allowBackup is false, so an image cannot be
pulled out with adb backup.


If you are shipping this
------------------------

RVM is young. Do not use it as the only isolation boundary for code you would not
otherwise run, and do not put its console or its network device on an untrusted
network without a host level sandbox around the process. A container, a seccomp
filter or Android's own app sandbox are all reasonable second layers, and none of
them need a change here.
